#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139725 :tutorial/content "The **difference of squares formula** states the product of the sum and the difference of two monomials is equal to the difference of their squares:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nWe can check that this result is correct by expanding the binomial in the usual way, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t({\\color{blue}a} + {\\color{red}b})(a - b) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}a}(a - b) + {\\color{red}b}(a - b) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\ta^2 - ab + ab - b^2 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\ta^2 - b^2 \\,& {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$\n\nNotice that the middle two terms $- ab$ and $ab$ cancel each other out, leaving only $a^2 - b^2.$ \n\nIf the order of multiplication is switched, the formula still remains the same:\n\n$$\n(a - b)(a + b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5aaa853f-8b04-5d91-8c05-f30646908746" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
